<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ercole I d'Este</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1431">1431</a>–<a href="/years/1505">1505</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Ercole I d'Este, Duke of Ferrara · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-este">House of Este</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ercole I d'Este was Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Modena</a> and Reggio from 1471 to 1505. A patron of the poet Boiardo, of music and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, he commissioned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/addizione-erculea">Addizione Erculea</a>, a planned extension of Ferrara. In the War of Ferrara against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> from 1482 to 1484 he lost the Polesine. His daughters Isabella and Beatrice married into the Gonzaga and Sforza families.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ercole-i-d-este.jpg" alt="Ercole I d'Este" width="320" height="421" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dosso Dossi / After Ercole de' Roberti · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ercole_I_d%27Este.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ercole was born on 26 October 1431 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a>, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niccolo-iii-deste">Niccolò III d'Este</a> and Ricciarda of Saluzzo. From 1445 to 1460 he was educated at the Neapolitan court of Alfonso, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, where he studied the military arts and chivalry and acquired a taste for architecture and the fine arts in the manner of antiquity.</p><p>In 1471 he became duke on the death of his half-brother Borso, with the support of the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, taking advantage of the absence in Mantua of Borso's son Niccolò. Niccolò later attempted a coup while Ercole was away from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a>; it was crushed, and Niccolò and his cousin Azzo were beheaded on 4 September 1476. In 1473 Ercole married Eleonora d'<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a>, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-i-of-naples">Ferdinand I of Naples</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>The War of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a>, fought from 1482 to 1484 against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, which was allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-sixtus-iv">Pope Sixtus IV</a>, arose from disputes over the salt monopoly. It ended with the Peace of Bagnolo. Ferrara escaped destruction or absorption into the papal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">dominions</a>, but the war was a humiliation for Ercole, who lay sick and immobilised while the besieging army destroyed Este properties around the city.</p><p>Afterwards he stayed neutral in the Italian War of 1494 to 1495 and tried to improve relations with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a>. He reluctantly agreed to the marriage of his son Alfonso to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lucrezia-borgia">Lucrezia Borgia</a>, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-alexander-vi">Pope Alexander VI</a>, which brought notable territorial gifts.</p><p>He admired the preacher <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girolamo-savonarola">Girolamo Savonarola</a>, also from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a>, and about a dozen letters between them survive from the 1490s. Ercole tried without success to have him freed by the Florentine church authorities; Savonarola was burned in 1498. In 1503 or 1504 Ercole asked his newly hired composer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josquin-des-prez">Josquin des Prez</a> for a musical testament, and the result was a Miserere built on Savonarola's prison meditation Infelix ego.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Ercole's patronage was in part a political and cultural statement, and he was the only Italian ruler to style himself divus on his coinage, like a Roman emperor. He staged plays with elaborate scenery and musical intermezzi, among the first purely secular <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> since antiquity, and built a musical establishment that for some years was the finest in Europe. Composers such as Alexander Agricola, Jacob Obrecht, Heinrich Isaac, Adrian Willaert and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josquin-des-prez">Josquin des Prez</a> worked for him or dedicated music to him; Josquin's Missa Hercules dux Ferrariae takes its theme from the syllables of the duke's name.</p><p>He brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ludovico-ariosto">Ludovico Ariosto</a> into his household. Under his direction <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a> roughly doubled in size, in a planned extension that was the first executed urban project of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>, with walls and buildings by the architect Biagio Rossetti. Ercole died on 25 January 1505, and his son Alfonso became duke.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ercole I d'Este” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Ercole I</b>'s planned extension of the city, the Addizione Erculea, was one of the first modern urban plans.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-ferrara">Duchy of Ferrara</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferrara">Ferrara</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.84,11.62&amp;year=1479" aria-label="Ferrara on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4827 579 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ercole I d'Este"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5277 849h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5277 849h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.84,11.62&amp;year=1479">What was here in 1479? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
